# Branding and legal assets: one canonical source for every project

All AIC PIM products in this workspace share one asset repository:

- **Local path:** `c:\analog-pim\core-assets`
- **Remote:** `https://github.com/analoginfo-pim/core-assets`

This is the **only** authoritative source for product branding (icons, logos,
splash images, header art, in-app bitmaps) and shared legal text (EULAs, license
files). Every other copy in the workspace is a *build output* of `core-assets` —
even when committed alongside source so `cargo build` / `tauri build` can find it.

The rule applies to **every** workspace root with no exceptions (`pim-orm*`,
`pim-app-config`, `pim-ui-kit`, all `pim-offline-*` server / client /
configurator / elevate crates, both product launchers, `pim-installers`, and any
future project). Add a new project's branded destinations to
[`core-assets/scripts/sync-to-projects.ps1`](../../core-assets/scripts/sync-to-projects.ps1)
*before* committing branded files into it.

## Hard rules

**R1. Never edit a branding file outside `core-assets`.** Covers `*.ico`,
`*.icns`, `*.svg`, `*.png` (logo / launcher / favicon variants), `EULA*.rtf`,
`License*.rtf`, `LICENSE*` used by installers, splash / header / about art, PWA
`manifest.json` icon entries, Apple Touch icons, and any branded resource
referenced by HTML / Tauri / WiX. Drift between an in-tree copy and
`core-assets` is a sync bug, not a legitimate per-project variant.

**R2. New resolutions or formats live in `core-assets` only.** When a project
needs a new size, format, or platform variant:

1. Add or extend the source under `core-assets/icons/source/` or
   `core-assets/branding/`.
2. Generate the derivative **inside `core-assets`** — `scripts/generate-icons.ps1`
   for raster-from-SVG, or
   `tauri icon core-assets/icons/source/aic-icon-1024.png --output core-assets/icons/tauri`
   for Tauri platform icons.
3. Commit and push **the source plus every generated artifact** to
   `core-assets`. That repository is the single physical home for the binary
   outputs — projects do not regenerate them locally.
4. Add the consumer destination to `scripts/sync-to-projects.ps1` and run the
   sync. The project then consumes the artifact as an external asset.

Forbidden: running `magick convert` / `inkscape` / `tauri icon` / `iconutil` or
any other generator inside a project repo and checking in the output; a project
build script that converts an asset on the fly into a different size or format,
even reading from a synced `core-assets` source; hand-editing a synced asset in
a project to tweak color, padding, or margin (tweak in `core-assets`, resync).

**R3. Add new shared assets to `core-assets` first**, under `icons/`,
`branding/`, `logos/`, or `legal/`, and only then sync into the consumer.
Extend `sync-to-projects.ps1` when you add a new consumer.

**R4. Never re-introduce a "local" branding fallback.** Build scripts point at
`core-assets` directly, or at the per-project copy the sync script populated.
Do not add a fallback chain searching a loose `deployment/windows/`,
`assets/branding/`, or similar drop directory — those uncontrolled paths are
exactly what puts the wrong logo on a customer's desktop.

**R5. Never commit a placeholder logo or stub license.** Generic "W"-shape Word
document icons, blank `License.rtf` stubs, and similar are forbidden even
temporarily, because build scripts pick them up and ship them. A non-AIC asset
needed for a test lives in `core-assets/icons/test/` with a documented reason.

Desktop / Start Menu shortcuts and PE icon resources must never ship the
noise/static placeholder family (`aic-header.PLACEHOLDER-do-not-use.png`, stale
Tauri `icon.ico` noise). See
[`no-noise-desktop-icons.mdc`](no-noise-desktop-icons.mdc).

## Adding a new branded artifact

1. Identify or add the source in `core-assets`: SVG vector →
   `icons/source/<name>.svg`; high-res master raster →
   `icons/source/<name>-<dim>.png`; in-app bitmap →
   `branding/<name>.png`; legal text → `legal/<name>.rtf`.
2. Generate every derivative consumers need, *inside `core-assets`*.
3. Commit and push source plus generated artifacts to `core-assets`.
4. Extend `scripts/sync-to-projects.ps1` with the consumer destinations.
5. Run the sync and commit the resulting copies in each consumer with a message
   like `chore(branding): sync from core-assets@<short-sha>`.

`core-assets/scripts/sync-to-projects.ps1` is the source of truth for the
who-consumes-what map (Tauri icon trees, 15+ `aic-icon.ico` destinations, web
favicon / manifest sets, `aic-header.png`, `aic-about.png`, and the commercial
EULA staged into MSIs). When in doubt, search that script for the file you are
about to touch; if it is listed, edit `core-assets` instead and run the sync.

## When agents must not do branding work

- If a requested change would require editing a branded file *outside*
  `core-assets`, push back: the edit belongs in `core-assets` followed by a
  sync. Ask for confirmation before bypassing the rule.
- A stale copy that disagrees with `core-assets` is **resynced from
  `core-assets`** — never "promoted" over the canonical file.
- A new icon size or format that does not yet exist is generated in
  `core-assets`, committed there, then synced. Never generated locally.
